<description>
Mark the shallowest and deepest columns of a solid terrain boundary with vertical colored guide lines.

Input structure:
- The first row is a solid empty separator.
- Below it, each column consists of upper fill cells followed by a contiguous suffix of terrain cells.
- The bottom row is all terrain, so every column has a well-defined first terrain row.

Rule:
- Find the column whose first terrain row is latest; it is unique and its upper fill run becomes maroon.
- Find the rightmost column whose first terrain row is earliest; its upper fill run becomes yellow.
- Preserve the empty separator, terrain cells, and all other upper fill cells.

Key insight:
- The task is a column-height summary of the terrain profile: color the deepest upper run and the highest terrain peak.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "columns below a solid empty top row, each with upper fill above a contiguous terrain suffix",
  "transform": "recolor the upper-fill run in the unique column with the latest terrain start to maroon; recolor the upper-fill run in the rightmost column with the earliest terrain start to yellow; preserve all other pixels"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 12x12 [observed]; solver requires >=2 rows and >=2 cols
  colors:
    empty: row 0 solid fill (0=black [observed])
    upper: rows 1..start-1 per column (1=blue [observed])
    terrain: contiguous suffix per column down to bottom (6=magenta [observed])
    output_new: maroon(9) and yellow(4) vertical stripes replacing upper fill
  terrain_profile:
    each column has a terrain height h = number of terrain cells (contiguous from bottom)
    adjacent column heights differ by 0-2 (observed max step 2, mean ~0.9)
    height range span 4-5 across columns
    min height 1-2, max height 5-7 (must leave >=1 upper cell in every column)
    bottom 1-2 rows are fully terrain [observed]
  constraints:
    unique_valley: exactly 1 column with minimum height (deepest upper run) -- required by solver assert
    peak_tie_ok: 1-2 columns may share maximum height; rightmost is chosen
    valley != peak column -- required by solver assert
  transform:
    maroon_col: unique valley column; upper fill (rows 1..start-1) recolored to maroon(9)
    yellow_col: rightmost peak column; upper fill (rows 1..start-1) recolored to yellow(4)
    all other cells unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (input is simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H x W (e.g. 10-14 each).
    2. Choose 3 distinct colors for empty, upper, terrain.
    3. Generate a terrain height profile h[0..W-1] with adjacent diffs in {-1,0,+1} (optionally allow +-2 rarely).
       Ensure all heights in [1, H-2], span >= 4.
    4. Ensure exactly one column has the global minimum height (unique valley).
       If multiple peaks share max height, that is ok (rightmost will be chosen).
       Ensure valley column != rightmost peak column.
    5. Fill grid -- row 0 = empty color, rows 1..H-1-h[c] = upper, rows H-h[c]..H-1 = terrain per column.
```
</generation_spec>
